{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that translates C++ code to Python 3 code. The code should reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Translate the following GNU C++11 code to Python 3 code. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your translated code should have the exact same functionality as the given code, meaning that it should have the same output for any given input.\nPrint your translated code and also the confidence score you give to your translation. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<translated_code>\n<your_translated_code>\n</translated_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the original GNU C++11 code you have to translate:\n<original_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <cstdint>\nusing namespace std;\ntypedef long long ll;\nconst int MOD = 1e9 + 7;\n\ninline void read(ll &x) {\n    x = 0; char c = getchar();\n    while (c < '0' || c > '9') c = getchar();\n    while (c >= '0' && c <= '9') { x = x * 10 + (c & 15); c = getchar(); }\n}\n\nstruct matrix {\n    ll a[35][35];\n    matrix() { memset(a, 0, sizeof(a)); }\n};\n\nmatrix mul(const matrix &x, const matrix &y, int sz) {\n    matrix z;\n    for (int i = 0; i <= sz; ++i)\n        for (int j = 0; j <= sz; ++j) {\n            ll s = 0;\n            for (int k = 0; k <= sz; ++k)\n                s = (s + x.a[i][k] * y.a[k][j]) % MOD;\n            z.a[i][j] = s;\n        }\n    return z;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ll n, m;\n    read(n); read(m);\n    ll tmp = n;\n    int cnt = -1;\n    ll dig[75];\n    while (tmp) {\n        dig[++cnt] = tmp % m;\n        tmp /= m;\n    }\n    matrix f[75][35];\n    matrix g;\n    g.a[0][m] = 1;\n    for (int j = 0; j < m; ++j) {\n        for (int i = 0; i <= m; ++i) {\n            f[0][j].a[i][j] = 1;\n            f[0][j].a[i][i] = 1;\n        }\n    }\n    for (int i = 1; i <= cnt; ++i) {\n        matrix pre[35], suf[35];\n        pre[0] = f[i-1][0];\n        for (int j = 1; j < m; ++j)\n            pre[j] = mul(pre[j-1], f[i-1][j], m);\n        suf[m-1] = f[i-1][m-1];\n        for (int j = m-2; j >= 0; --j)\n            suf[j] = mul(f[i-1][j], suf[j+1], m);\n        f[i][0] = suf[0];\n        for (int j = 1; j < m; ++j)\n            f[i][j] = mul(suf[j], pre[j-1], m);\n    }\n    int cur = 0;\n    for (int i = cnt; i >= 0; --i) {\n        while (dig[i]--) {\n            g = mul(g, f[i][cur], m);\n            cur = (cur + 1) % m;\n        }\n    }\n    ll ans = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i <= m; ++i)\n        ans = (ans + g.a[0][i]) % MOD;\n    cout << ans << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<translated_code>\nimport sys\n\nMOD = 10**9 + 7\n\ndef read_ints():\n    return map(int, sys.stdin.read().split())\n\ndef mul(x, y, sz):\n    z = [[0] * (sz + 1) for _ in range(sz + 1)]\n    for i in range(sz + 1):\n        for j in range(sz + 1):\n            s = 0\n            for k in range(sz + 1):\n                s = (s + x[i][k] * y[k][j]) % MOD\n            z[i][j] = s\n    return z\n\ndef main():\n    data = list(read_ints())\n    if not data:\n        return\n    n, m = data[0], data[1]\n    tmp = n\n    cnt = -1\n    dig = []\n    while tmp:\n        dig.append(tmp % m)\n        tmp //= m\n        cnt += 1\n    # dig now has least significant first, but we need most significant first later\n    # In C++ code, dig[0] is most significant? Actually they fill from index 0 with most significant? Let's check:\n    # while(tmp) { dig[++cnt] = tmp % m; tmp /= m; } So first digit stored is least significant? No: ++cnt increments first, so first iteration cnt=0, stores least significant digit. So dig[0] is least significant, dig[cnt] is most significant. In later 